86 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 55 0 R
/E (In practice, many households and small businesses continue to struggle with affordability, reliability, and the practical value of the energy available to them. This raises an important question. If energy infrastructure is expanding, why is impact still limited?)
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
87 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 55 0 R
/E <>
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
88 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 55 0 R
/E (Introduction)
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
89 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 55 0 R
/E (FROM ACCESS TO UTILIZATION 2026)
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
90 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /Figure
/P 56 0 R
/Pg 13 0 R
/A [215 0 R 0]
/K [0]
>>
endobj
91 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 56 0 R
/E (One of the most important insights from the dialogue was the growing disconnect between energy access and energy use. While infrastructure such as mini grids and solar systems is being deployed, actual usage often remains low.)
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
92 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 56 0 R
/E (A clear example is a mini grid installation in Ada where only about one third of the community was actively using the system one year after commissioning. This is not an isolated case. It reflects a broader issue where energy systems are introduced without fully aligning with the economic realities and needs of the communities they are meant to serve.)
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
93 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 56 0 R
/E (Access, in this sense, becomes a starting point rather than an outcome. Without consistent and meaningful use, energy systems cannot deliver the economic and social benefits they are designed for.)
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
94 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 56 0 R
/E (From Access to Utilization)
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
