<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 56 0 R
/E (FROM ACCESS TO UTILIZATION 2026)
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
96 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /Figure
/P 56 0 R
/Pg 13 0 R
/A [216 0 R 0]
/K [6]
>>
endobj
97 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /Figure
/P 56 0 R
/Pg 13 0 R
/A [217 0 R 0]
/K [7]
>>
endobj
98 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /Figure
/P 57 0 R
/Pg 14 0 R
/A [218 0 R 0]
/K [0]
>>
endobj
99 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 57 0 R
/E (Ghana is not short of renewable energy solutions. Technologies such as solar home systems, mini grids, and clean cooking solutions are already available and increasingly accessible. The real constraint lies in how these solutions are financed and sustained.)
/Pg 14 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
100 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 57 0 R
/E (Current financing approaches include pay as you go systems, mini grid investments, and blended finance models that combine grants, concessional loans, and private capital. While these models have enabled progress, they have not been sufficient to scale solutions across rural communities.)
/Pg 14 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
101 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 57 0 R
/E (Several challenges continue to limit investment. Financial institutions often perceive renewable energy projects as high risk, particularly due to currency volatility between the Ghana cedi and major foreign currencies. In addition, many projects struggle to demonstrate bankability because of limited data on demand, usage patterns, and creditworthiness.)
/Pg 14 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
102 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 57 0 R
/E (As a result, a significant financing gap remains, preventing many promising projects from moving beyond pilot stages.)
/Pg 14 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
103 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 57 0 R
/E (The Real Constraint: Financing, Not Technology)
/Pg 14 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
104 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 57 0 R
/E (FROM ACCESS TO UTILIZATION 2026)
/Pg 14 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
105 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /Figure
/P 58 0 R
/Pg 15 0 R
/A [219 0 R 0]
/K [0]
>>
endobj
106 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 58 0 R
/E (An often overlooked consequence of underutilized energy systems is its broader economic and environmental impact. When renewable energy systems are not fully used, their potential benefits are not realized. At the same time, households continue to rely on traditional energy sources such as charcoal, firewood, and diesel generators.)
/Pg 15 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
107 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 58 0 R
/E (This creates a situation where clean energy exists alongside continued use of high emission fuels. In effect, the presence of renewable energy infrastructure does not automatically lead to a reduction in emissions.)
/Pg 15 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
108 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 58 0 R
/E (A system that is not actively used does not displace existing energy practices. Instead, it delays the transition to cleaner energy. This highlights an important point. The effectiveness of renewable energy investments should not be measured only by installation, but by actual and sustained use.)
/Pg 15 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
109 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 58 0 R
/E (The Hidden Cost of Underutilization)
/Pg 15 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
110 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 58 0 R
/E (FROM ACCESS TO UTILIZATION 2026)
/Pg 15 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
111 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /Figure
/P 59 0 R
/Pg 16 0 R
/A [220 0 R 0]
/K [0]
>>
endobj
112 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 59 0 R
/E (A key takeaway from the dialogue was the importance of reducing risk in order to unlock financing. While various financing tools exist, their effectiveness depends on how well risks are managed and shared.)
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
113 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 59 0 R
/E (Blended finance plays an important role by combining public and private resources. Grants and concessional loans can help lower the financial burden on projects and make them more attractive to investors. Carbon finance also offers an additional revenue stream by allowing projects to generate and sell carbon credits.)
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
114 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 59 0 R
/E (Another promising approach is results based financing, where payments are tied to verified outcomes such as the number of operational systems or active users. This creates incentives for quality and long term performance rather than short term installation targets.)
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
115 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 59 0 R
/E (However, for these mechanisms to work at scale, there is a need for stronger coordination and implementation. In particular, the Renewable Energy Fund must move beyond policy and become a fully operational and well capitalized facility that can effectively support projects and share risks with investors.)
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
116 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 59 0 R
/E (De Risking as the Missing Link)
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
117 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 59 0 R
/E (FROM ACCESS TO UTILIZATION 2026)
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
118 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /Figure
/P 60 0 R
/Pg 17 0 R
/A [221 0 R 0]
/K [0]
>>
endobj
119 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 60 0 R
/E (One of the most practical insights from the discussion was the need to connect energy access with productive use. Energy systems are more likely to be adopted and sustained when they directly support income generating activities.)
/Pg 17 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
120 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 60 0 R
/E (For example, solar powered equipment for agriculture, small scale processing, or local businesses can increase productivity and create new economic opportunities. When communities see clear financial benefits, their willingness to adopt and consistently use energy solutions increases significantly.)
/Pg 17 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
121 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 60 0 R
/E (This approach shifts energy from being a basic service to being an enabler of economic growth. It also improves the financial viability of projects by creating more predictable demand and revenue streams.)
/Pg 17 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
122 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 60 0 R
/E (Linking Energy )
/Pg 17 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
123 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 60 0 R
/E (to Livelihoods)
/Pg 17 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
124 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 60 0 R
/E (FROM ACCESS TO UTILIZATION 2026)
/Pg 17 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
125 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /Figure
/P 61 0 R
/Pg 18 0 R
/A [222 0 R 0]
/K [0]
>>
endobj
126 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /Figure
/P 61 0 R
/Pg 18 0 R
/A [223 0 R 0]
/K [1]
>>
endobj
127 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 61 0 R
/E (ALLIANCE FOR EMPOWERING RURAL COMMUNITIES)
/Pg 18 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
128 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 61 0 R
/E (Ghana has made important progress in expanding access to electricity, but the next phase of the energy transition requires a shift in focus. The challenge is no longer only about connecting communities to energy systems. It is about ensuring that these systems are used, valued, and sustained over time.)
/Pg 18 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
129 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 61 0 R
/E (Addressing this challenge requires rethinking how renewable energy is financed and implemented. Greater attention must be given to de risking investments, linking energy to livelihoods, and ensuring that systems are designed with the realities of users in mind.)
/Pg 18 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
